Let $\lambda_1(\Omega, \alpha)$ be the lowest Robin eigenvalue on a bounded domain $\Omega$ with a negative boundary parameter $\alpha$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item In two dimensions, the disk maximizes $\lambda_1(\Omega, \alpha)$ among all simply connected domains of the same area.
\item In three and higher dimensions, the ball maximizes $\lambda_1(\Omega, \alpha)$ among all convex domains of the same volume.
\end{enumerate}

Among all triangles of a given area $\lambda_1(\Omega, \alpha)$ is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item minimized by the equilateral triangle when $\alpha>0$.
\item maximized by the equilateral triangle when $\alpha<0$.
\end{enumerate}

exact_source.status_html:
The conjecture is settled by G. Polya for $N=3, 4$ via Steiner symmetrization. However, if $N\geq 5$ symmetrization might increase the number of edges of a polygon and consequently this case requires a different approach. For all $N$, it is known that minimizers exist and have precisely $N$ edges; see Theorem 3.3.1  in \cite{MR2251558}

Interesting partial questions and further problems are for instance:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that the regular $N$-gon is a local minimizer.
\item Prove that a minimizer must be convex.
\item Prove that $\lambda_1(\Omega_N)$ of the regular $N$-gon $\Omega_N$ is decreasing as a function of $N$. This is a necessary condition for the validity of the conjecture (remarked by C. Nitsch).
\item Consider the corresponding problem, but for the maximization of the first non-trivial Neumann eigenvalue. 
\item Consider the corresponding problems but with the perimeter constraint. 
\end{enumerate}
For the Neumann problem, the case $N=3$ was solved by R. Laugesen and B. Siudeja \cite{MR2567204} for both area and perimeter constraints, but for $N\geq 4$ the problem remains open.

